    <!-- 6. The desktop, and control that's safe for a moving machine (docs/22, docs/10, docs/15) -->
    <section class="wrap">
      <h2>The machine's desktop <small>And control that's safe for one that moves</small></h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The desktop your machine already has, in your dashboard — no tool for the
        end customer to install. <span class="badge dev">in development</span>
      </p>
      <ul class="features">
        <li>Unattended: back after a reboot with nobody at the machine</li>
        <li>Every monitor, plugged in or out while you watch</li>
        <li>Virtual screens for machines with no monitor attached</li>
        <li>The clipboard both ways</li>
        <li>Typing through the machine's own keyboard layout</li>
        <li>Its own cursor, drawn where it really is</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 class="sub">Who's in control is explicit</h3>
      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card"><span class="ic"><FjIcon name="stop" /></span><h3>Taking control stops the robot first</h3><p>Driving is one operator at a time, and never handed over on idle. Taking it over releases every input before anyone new can move it.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="ic"><FjIcon name="pointer" /></span><h3>One pointer, handed over cleanly</h3><p>Desktop control frees five seconds after the holder's last input, or anyone can take it at once — and the holder is told.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="ic"><FjIcon name="heartbeat" /></span><h3>Gone means released</h3><p>If an operator's connection dies, their held keys and buttons are released and their control lapses. A machine is never left stuck.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="ic"><FjIcon name="eye" /></span><h3>Watch without touching</h3><p>A view-only grant can never take control or read the clipboard — for the customer, the auditor, the second pair of eyes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="note">Each of these ships with a regression test (<a href="/15-testing-strategy/#safety-behaviors-safety-behaviors">safety behaviors</a>). How control works: <a href="/10-security/">security</a> · <a href="/22-remote-desktop-client/">remote desktop</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 7. A link, not a VPN (docs/03#tunnel-positioning, docs/27, docs/29) -->
    <section class="wrap">
      <h2>A link to one machine <small>Not a VPN</small></h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Every team keeps a list of things it occasionally needs to do on a machine
        that nobody will ever write a protocol for. The network link is the escape
        hatch: an authorized operator gets a routable address for <b>one</b> machine, inside the session that already carries its video — and the tools
        they already use just work.
      </p>
      <Code lang="console" code={connectSession} />
      <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:16px;">
        {tunnelUses.map((u) => (
          <div class="card">
            <h3>{u.title}</h3>
            <p>{u.desc}</p>
            <Code code={u.code} lang={u.lang} frame="none" />
            <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:0.875rem;"><a href={u.href}>How-to →</a></p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="note">And ssh, scp, RDP, your file manager, Wireshark, a serial port, Modbus — <a href="/28-tunnel-ideas/">more ideas</a>.</p>
      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <div class="card"><span class="ic"><FjIcon name="hub" /></span><h3>Isolation you can't misconfigure</h3><p>Connect to two machines at once and they still can't see each other. Every link ends at you and nothing is forwarded between them — there's no path to leave open by mistake.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="ic"><FjIcon name="ticket" /></span><h3>One set of permissions</h3><p>You already decide who may reach machine 42. The link is that same decision, in the same grant, in the same audit log. No second identity system to run beside yours.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="ic"><FjIcon name="clock" /></span><h3>Access that expires</h3><p>The link exists while a session does, then it's gone. Nothing becomes permanently reachable, and there's no extra daemon on the machine to install, update or license.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        It deliberately isn't a mesh: no shared network, no machine-to-machine
        routing, no always-on reachability. It's developer and support access — <a href="/27-network-tunnel/">the design</a> says exactly where that line sits.
      </p>
    </section>

